WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
Jennifer Pinches: Good morning, John. It's Friday.

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:07.540
Hank: So, after the general confusion, and jetlag, and delicacies, and all the tiny yet somehow overwhelming

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:14.440
differences of London, - "My world is exploding!" - we went to Dublin, where I was finally

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:20.940
and truly hit with that same tired, old realization that all Americans have upon travelling to Europe:

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:24.040
Dang. This place is old.

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:31.500
But also, like, new! Like, super new. This sense of extreme juxtaposition. Old right across the river from new.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:41.900
Old boat, new bridge, old stairs, new stairs, old security system, new security system, old obelisk, new obelisk.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:45.280
It seemed to me like two categories with a fine line between them,

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.300
and I know to some people that will make me seem like some kind of barbarian,

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:54.840
but if you were raised in a place where the oldest building was built in the 1950s, you might lack some historical perspective, too.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:01.260
But then, as we traveled around, popping into every single book shop to see if they had, 'The Fault in our Stars'...

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.640
Hank: "What were you just doin', John?"

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:04.840
John: "Videotaping my own book."

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.060
Dublin started to teach us about itself.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.640
A cathedral that took so long to construct that Romanesque arches sit right next to Gothic arches.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:19.120
Two architectural styles as different and separated by as many years as this building and this one,

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.680
with a crypt underneath it predating both of those architectural styles.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:27.880
In fact, the oldest structure in Dublin, though with some decidedly new uses.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:33.100
In addition to having a museum, there's also a gift shop, a coffee shop, and some toilets.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:37.980
But of course Dublin's history reaches back a lot further than that as we learned at Dublinia.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.280
A celebration of Dublin's founding as a Viking colony.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:46.120
Which I mention mostly, so I can show you this Viking pooping...

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.040
*Pooping Viking makes groaning sound of relief*

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.580
But then a little more robustly, I peeked into Ireland's very long history at the National Museum of Archaeology,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:59.640
and suddenly, this intense cultural continuity came into a weird sort of focus for me,

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.840
with these ax heads being carved 5,800 years before these broaches were molded,

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.560
two centuries before Strongbow died,

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.760
400 years before this library was founded,

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.600
300 years before this primitive means of wired communication was invented,

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.520
1,600 years before this fossilized bog mummy died,

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.780
400 years before these other two bog mummies died,

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:30.740
almost 2,000 years before this cat chased this rat into a pipe organ in Christ Church Cathedral, where they both together were also mummified,

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.700
300 years before this gutter was installed,

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.800
14 years before this event was crazy,

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:40.500
and more than 1,200 years before the first Viking...

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.020
pooped on Ireland.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:43.800
*Pooping Viking makes groaning sound of relief*

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:50.200
We only have a concept of self because of our ability to symbolically recall our past and stitch together a story of who we are.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.260
In many ways, we are never the same physically or psychologically from instant to instant,

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.900
it's that continuity that makes us, us.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:02.320
That same thing could be said of a place, or of a culture, or even of a species.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.820
Yes, at any given moment, Dublin is a different city than it was the moment before,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:12.380
but while it at first looked like a city of juxtaposition, it became a continuum.

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:20.780
A continuum that was respected, and honored, and that reached back tens of thousands of years, and that I felt like I was a part of.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:28.020
Without that conscientiousness of our past, which is very easy not to have for those of us who live in towns that have barely existed for 100 years,

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:34.140
it's easy to forget that we're not only living in this particular instant, but as part of a continuum,

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:37.740
thousands, or tens of thousands, or even millions of years old.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:41.380
And without that perspective I think we might be a little bit culturally stunted.

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:45.600
So basically, what I'm saying, is watch Crash Course World History,

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:47.260
and Vikings pooped, too.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.840
*Pooping Viking makes groaning sound of relief*

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:54.320
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.200
*Pooping Viking makes groaning sound of relief*

